<?xml version="1.0"?>
<document>
  <properties>
    <title>Publishing release-specific POMs from local parent templates</title>
  </properties>

  <body>
    <section name="Publishing release-specific POMs from local parent templates">
      <p>
        A local parent can be more than a place to share static configuration. It can be a build-time template whose
        dependencies, imported BOMs, plugins, compiler settings, and source roots are parameterized by a thin leaf POM.
        Different leaves can select different Java and library generations while the common configuration remains in
        one place. Set <code>flattenRelativePathParent</code> to bake each selection into its installed or deployed POM.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Maven reactor still builds the original hierarchy from disk. For publication, the plugin merges the raw
        local parent chain into each child and retains the first external parent. The consumer receives a conventional,
        self-contained POM with concrete line-specific properties and no references to the repository's build-only
        structure.
      </p>

      <source><![CDATA[
BUILD TIME                                  INSTALL / DEPLOY

external published parent                  external published parent
            ^                                           ^
repository parent (local template)                      |
  uses leaf version properties              published release-line artifact
            ^                               + selected Java/framework properties
module parent (local template)              + inherited dependencies and plugins
  shared dependencies/plugins
            ^
thin release-line leaf
  supplies Java, framework and library versions

The source hierarchy and reactor do not change. Only each published child POM is collapsed.
      ]]></source>

      <subsection name="Three framework lines across two JDKs">
        <p>
          A parent POM or BOM often needs parallel release lines for consumers on different framework generations.
          Keep the dependency and plugin declarations in one local template. Each thin leaf supplies a compatible
          version set: for example framework 4 on Java 21, framework 5.0 on Java 25, and framework 5.1 on Java 25.
          Flattening publishes the same artifact identity in disjoint version namespaces.
        </p>
        <source><![CDATA[
common-parent.xml (local template)
  imports framework-bom:${framework.version}
  configures compiler for ${java.version}
  configures framework plugin ${framework.plugin.version}
               |
       +-------+----------------+----------------+
       |                        |                |
pom.xml                    pom-v5.xml       pom-v51.xml
Java 21                    Java 25          Java 25
framework 4                framework 5.0    framework 5.1
version 1.4                version v5-1.4   version v51-1.4
       |                        |                |
       +---------------- flatten each -----------+
                                |
              three self-contained published POMs
              with concrete, mutually compatible versions
        ]]></source>
        <p>
          The template may use properties declared only by the leaf. It does not have to resolve as an independent
          published model because the plugin merges the raw models before flattening the selected line.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <subsection name="One library, different JDK implementations and adapters">
        <p>
          A library can share most of its sources while selecting a small JDK-specific implementation per line. A
          module-local parent owns the source-selection plugin, and each leaf chooses its compiler release and source
          root. A separate adapter parent can use the same pattern to select the matching framework BOM and plugin
          generation.
        </p>
        <source><![CDATA[
repository parent (local)
|
+-- core parent (local)
|   shared API, tests and source-selection plugin
|   |
|   +-- Java 21 leaf  -- src/main/java21 --> published core line
|   +-- Java 22+ leaf -- src/main/java22 --> published core line
|
+-- adapter parent (local)
    shared framework dependencies and plugins
    |
    +-- Java 21 / framework 4 --> published adapter line
    +-- Java 25 / framework 5 --> published adapter line

Consumers choose a coherent line; the repository keeps one shared build definition.
        ]]></source>
      </subsection>

      <subsection name="A multi-module compatibility matrix">
        <p>
          Larger reactors can layer repository-wide platform configuration with module-specific parents for database,
          broker, HTTP, or test-container support. Release aggregators select the leaf POM for each line; every leaf
          carries that line's Java and BOM properties. The local parents keep shared backend dependencies, test
          plugins, and lifecycle setup out of the thin variants.
        </p>
        <source><![CDATA[
release aggregator A                         release aggregator B
selects */pom.xml                            selects */pom-v5.xml
for Java 21 / framework 4                   for Java 25 / framework 5
          |                                           |
          +---------------+   +-----------------------+
                          |   |
repository parent (local; imports ${framework.bom.version})
|
+-- core common parent --------> core leaf A       core leaf B
+-- database common parent ----> database leaf A   database leaf B
+-- broker common parent ------> broker leaf A     broker leaf B
+-- test common parent --------> test leaf A       test leaf B
                                  |                 |
                                  +-- flatten each -+

Published modules contain their selected platform and inherited module setup.
Build-only verification modules can still opt out of install or deploy normally.
        ]]></source>
        <p>
          This separates two Maven roles cleanly: aggregators choose which variants enter a reactor, while parents
          provide reusable configuration. <code>flattenRelativePathParent</code> removes the local inheritance
          scaffolding from the consumer-facing result without changing either role during the build.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <subsection name="How the local parent chain is collapsed">
        <p>
          The following complete example shows the underlying mechanism with a repository-wide parent, a
          module-specific parent, and one published child.
        </p>
      </subsection>

      <subsection name="Repository-wide parent">
        <p>
          The repository-wide parent has fixed coordinates and inherits from a stable published parent. Its empty
          <code>relativePath</code> marks the end of the local chain. It also configures the flatten plugin once for
          every project using this layout.
        </p>
        <source><![CDATA[
<project>
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
  <parent>
    <groupId>com.example</groupId>
    <artifactId>published-parent</artifactId>
    <version>2.0.0</version>
    <relativePath/>
  </parent>

  <groupId>com.example</groupId>
  <artifactId>repository-common-parent</artifactId>
  <version>LOCAL</version>
  <packaging>pom</packaging>

  <properties>
    <repository.property>repository-value</repository.property>
  </properties>

  <dependencyManagement>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.example</groupId>
        <artifactId>platform-bom</artifactId>
        <version>${platform.version}</version>
        <type>pom</type>
        <scope>import</scope>
      </dependency>
    </dependencies>
  </dependencyManagement>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
        <artifactId>flatten-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <flattenMode>resolveCiFriendliesOnly</flattenMode>
          <flattenRelativePathParent>true</flattenRelativePathParent>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>flatten</id>
            <phase>process-resources</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>flatten</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
          <execution>
            <id>flatten.clean</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>clean</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
        ]]></source>
      </subsection>

      <subsection name="Module-specific parent">
        <p>
          A second repository-only parent can add configuration for one module or a group of modules. Its parent is
          resolved from another source-tree POM, so both local layers will be collapsed.
        </p>
        <source><![CDATA[
<project>
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
  <parent>
    <groupId>com.example</groupId>
    <artifactId>repository-common-parent</artifactId>
    <version>LOCAL</version>
    <relativePath>../common-pom.xml</relativePath>
  </parent>

  <groupId>com.example</groupId>
  <artifactId>library-common-parent</artifactId>
  <version>LOCAL</version>
  <packaging>pom</packaging>

  <properties>
    <module.property>module-value</module.property>
  </properties>
</project>
        ]]></source>
      </subsection>

      <subsection name="Published child">
        <p>
          The child points to its nearest repository-only parent explicitly. The coordinates in every local parent
          reference must match the POM found at its <code>relativePath</code>.
        </p>
        <source><![CDATA[
<project>
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
  <parent>
    <groupId>com.example</groupId>
    <artifactId>library-common-parent</artifactId>
    <version>LOCAL</version>
    <relativePath>common-pom.xml</relativePath>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>example-library</artifactId>
  <version>${revision}</version>
  <properties>
    <platform.version>4.2.0</platform.version>
  </properties>
</project>
        ]]></source>

        <p>The installed or deployed POM contains both inherited property sets and the published parent:</p>
        <source><![CDATA[
<project>
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
  <parent>
    <groupId>com.example</groupId>
    <artifactId>published-parent</artifactId>
    <version>2.0.0</version>
  </parent>

  <groupId>com.example</groupId>
  <artifactId>example-library</artifactId>
  <version>1.0.0</version>
  <properties>
    <repository.property>repository-value</repository.property>
    <module.property>module-value</module.property>
    <platform.version>4.2.0</platform.version>
  </properties>
  <dependencyManagement>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.example</groupId>
        <artifactId>platform-bom</artifactId>
        <version>${platform.version}</version>
        <type>pom</type>
        <scope>import</scope>
      </dependency>
    </dependencies>
  </dependencyManagement>
</project>
        ]]></source>
        <p>
          The flattened child does not reference either repository-only parent, and the
          <code>flattenRelativePathParent</code> control flag is removed from every flattened POM. An empty
          <code>relativePath</code>, a missing local POM, or mismatched local coordinates ends the local chain and leaves
          that parent for repository resolution. This also allows the inherited execution to run safely on the
          repository-only parents when they are included in the reactor.
        </p>
      </subsection>
    </section>
  </body>
</document>
